1. Problem Statement :

We have an array heights, where each index represents a stair and the value represents its height.

The frog starts at index 0 and wants to reach the last index.

The frog can jump at most k stairs at a time.

The cost of jumping from stair a to stair b is:

|heights[a] - heights[b]|

We need to find the minimum total cost to reach the last stair.



2. Intuition :

Think about reaching any stair i.

Since the frog can jump up to k positions, it could reach i from:

i-1
i-2
i-3
...
i-k

So I ask:

"From which previous stair should I jump to i so that my total cost is minimum?"

For every possible previous stair, I calculate:

cost to reach previous stair
+
cost of current jump

Then I choose the minimum.



3. Logic :

The frog starts from the first stair and can jump at most K stairs at a time. The cost of each jump is the absolute difference between the heights of the two stairs.

I use dynamic programming where dp[i] represents the minimum cost required to reach stair i.

For every stair, I try all possible jumps from 1 to K. For each jump, I calculate the cost of reaching the previous stair plus the cost of the current jump. I take the minimum among all these possibilities and store it in dp[i].

Finally, dp[n-1] gives the minimum cost required to reach the last stair




4. Code :

class Solution {
    public int frogJump(int[] heights, int k) {
        int[] dp=new int[heights.length];
        dp[0]=0;
        if(heights.length==1){
            return 0;
        }
        dp[1]=Math.abs(heights[0]-heights[1]);
        for(int i=2;i<heights.length;i++){
            dp[i]=Integer.MAX_VALUE;
            for(int j=1;j<=k && i-j>-1;j++){
            dp[i]=Math.min(Math.abs(heights[i]-heights[i-j])+dp[i-j],dp[i]);
            }
        }
        System.out.print(Arrays.toString(dp));
        return dp[heights.length-1];
    }
}
